For a partially ordered set $X$, a subset $U \subseteq X$ is open in the *induced topology* when it satisfies:

(i) whenever $x \in U$ and $x \sqsubseteq y$, then $y \in U$;

(ii) whenever $S \subseteq X$ is *directed*, $\bigsqcup S$ exists, and $\bigsqcup S \in U$, then $S \cap U \neq \emptyset$.

Directed sets are non-empty. The sets satisfying (i) and (ii) are the open sets of the induced topology.

`ScottOpen` is that predicate on a complete lattice. `ScottOpen.isUpperSet` is (i). `ScottOpen.univ`, `ScottOpen.inter`, and `ScottOpen.sUnion` are the topology laws: the universe is open, binary intersections are open, and arbitrary unions are open.

The discussion that leads to (i) and (ii) is [Specialization](../Specialization/1972_Specialization.md).
